{
  "master_id": "pat-martino",
  "work_id": "linear-expressions",
  "usage_notes": [
    {
      "chord_quality": "m7",
      "function_role": "minor-tonic-activity-anchor",
      "narrative": "For minor seventh chords, Martino prescribes identifying the chord's inversion form (1-4) and selecting the corresponding numbered activity: Form 1 maps to Activities 1 and 5, Form 2 to Activity 2, Form 3 to Activity 3, Form 4 to Activity 4. The chord inversion is the operative selector -- melodic choice follows from it rather than from independent judgment. Chapter 1, p. 10.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:linear-expressions:chord-activity-mapping/chord-inversion-selects-activity"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "linear-expressions",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "linear-expressions",
          "citation": "chapter 1 p.10"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "m7",
      "function_role": "positional-key-assignment",
      "narrative": "For minor seventh chords, Martino prescribes determining the key not from the chord name alone but from the position on the neck where the activity form is played: the same activity shape in third position is G minor, in fifth position is A minor, etc. Position is the key selector; the form is key-neutral. Chapter 1, p. 11.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:linear-expressions:chord-activity-mapping/position-determines-key"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "linear-expressions",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "linear-expressions",
          "citation": "chapter 1 p.11"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "m7",
      "function_role": "cyclic-octave-wraparound",
      "narrative": "For minor seventh chords encountered after completing all five activity forms, Martino prescribes cycling back to Activity 1 one octave higher rather than stopping -- Activities 1 and 5 share the same chord inversion (Form 1) and the same sixth-string starting note (G), making the system infinitely repeatable up the neck. Chapter 1, p. 15.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:linear-expressions:chord-activity-mapping/cyclic-octave-wraparound"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "linear-expressions",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "linear-expressions",
          "citation": "chapter 1 p.15"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "i",
      "granularity_name": "other"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "m7",
      "function_role": "horizontal-transposition-prerequisite",
      "narrative": "For minor seventh chords, Martino prescribes mastering all five activity forms horizontally in all twelve keys before advancing to Phase II (vertical movement). The Phase I completion rule is explicit and non-negotiable: 'Then and only then should you move onto PHASE II.' Chapter 1, p. 16.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:linear-expressions:chord-activity-mapping/position-determines-key"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "linear-expressions",
      "references": [
