      "polarity": "proscriptive",
      "cross_ref": [
        "dom7alt/scale-choice-over-altered-dominant"
      ],
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "m7b5",
      "function_role": "minor-via-minor-third-substitution",
      "narrative": "For half-diminished (m7b5) chords, Martino prescribes moving up a minor third from the m7b5 root and playing minor -- e.g., Bm7b5 -> Dm6 (up a m3). The rationale is explicit: m7b5 is an incomplete dominant ninth chord and is inversionally equivalent to m6 in third inversion, making the substitution harmonically coherent rather than approximate. Chapter 4, p. 55.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:linear-expressions:harmonic-substitution-reduction/m7b5-minor-third-up-rule"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "linear-expressions",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "linear-expressions",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p.55"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "m7b5",
      "function_role": "inversion-equivalence-with-m6",
      "narrative": "For half-diminished chords, Martino prescribes understanding their inversional equivalence to m6: Bm7b5 is Dm6 in third inversion; conversely, Dm6 is Bm7b5 in first inversion. This bi-directional equivalence is the conceptual foundation for the m7b5-to-minor substitution rule and explains why the same activities apply across both chord types. Chapter 4, p. 55.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:linear-expressions:harmonic-substitution-reduction/m7b5-minor-third-up-rule"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "linear-expressions",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "linear-expressions",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p.55"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "aug",
      "function_role": "minor-maj7-via-major-sixth-substitution",
      "narrative": "For augmented triads, Martino prescribes going up a major sixth from the root and playing melodic or harmonic minor (preferring melodic minor). Because every augmented triad has three enharmonic root names, three distinct minor-maj7 substitutions are available from a single chord -- e.g., F augmented yields Dm(maj7), F#m(maj7), and Bbm(maj7). Martino provides a diagram showing each enharmonic root name and its corresponding minor-maj7 target. Chapter 4, p. 56.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:linear-expressions:harmonic-substitution-reduction/augmented-triad-major-sixth-up-rule"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "linear-expressions",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "linear-expressions",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p.56"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "aug",
      "function_role": "enharmonic-root-tripling",
      "narrative": "For augmented triads, Martino prescribes treating each of the triad's three notes as a valid root name -- the triad inverts to form two other augmented triads enharmonically, so F aug = A aug = Db aug. This tripling of available root names is the prerequisite step before applying the major-sixth-up minor-maj7 substitution rule. Chapter 4, p. 55.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:linear-expressions:harmonic-substitution-reduction/augmented-triad-major-sixth-up-rule"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "linear-expressions",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "linear-expressions",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p.55"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj",
      "function_role": "slash-chord-relative-minor-substitution",
      "narrative": "For major triads appearing as slash chords (major triad over a bass note), Martino prescribes converting the major triad to its relative minor and reinterpreting the bass note as a scale degree of that minor key. For example, C/F# becomes Am with the F# treated as the 6th of A minor. Chapter 4, p. 56.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:linear-expressions:harmonic-substitution-reduction/relative-minor-reduction"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "linear-expressions",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "linear-expressions",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p.56"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj",
      "function_role": "slash-chord-secondary-relative-minor-fallback",
      "narrative": "For major triad slash chords where the primary relative minor clashes with the bass note (e.g., creating a b5 in the bass), Martino prescribes falling back to the secondary relative minor -- the III chord of the major key. For Db/E, Bb minor is rejected because E creates a b5 against it; F minor (III of Db major) is prescribed instead. The F harmonic minor scale is validated by containing all pitch content of the slash chord. Chapter 4, p. 56.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:linear-expressions:harmonic-substitution-reduction/secondary-relative-minor-fallback"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "linear-expressions",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "linear-expressions",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p.56"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "cross_ref": [
        "maj/slash-chord-relative-minor-proscription"
      ],
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj",
      "function_role": "slash-chord-b5-bass-clash-avoidance",
      "narrative": "Do not apply the primary relative minor substitution to a major triad slash chord when the bass note creates a b5 against the substitute minor root -- this interval clash is explicitly cited as the disqualifying condition that forces the secondary relative minor fallback. Chapter 4, p. 56.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:linear-expressions:harmonic-substitution-reduction/secondary-relative-minor-fallback"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "linear-expressions",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "linear-expressions",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p.56"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "polarity": "proscriptive",
      "cross_ref": [
        "maj/slash-chord-secondary-relative-minor-fallback"
      ],
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "m6",
      "function_role": "inversion-equivalence-with-m7b5",
      "narrative": "For minor sixth chords, Martino prescribes understanding their inversional equivalence to m7b5: Dm6 is Bm7b5 in first inversion. The equivalence table in Chapter 3 also shows Cm7b5 = Ebm6, making m6 and m7b5 freely interchangeable within the substitution system. Chapter 3, p. 50; Chapter 4, p. 55.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:linear-expressions:harmonic-substitution-reduction/m7b5-minor-third-up-rule"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "linear-expressions",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "linear-expressions",
          "citation": "chapter 3 p.50"
        },
        {
          "source": "linear-expressions",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p.55"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "m6",
      "function_role": "dominant-ninth-substitution-target",
      "narrative": "For minor sixth chords as substitution targets, Martino prescribes using them as the output form of the dominant-ninth reduction: G9 resolves to Dm6 (minor rooted a perfect fifth above G). The m6 form is the preferred minor target -- not plain m7 -- when reducing a dominant ninth. Chapter 4, p. 55.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:linear-expressions:harmonic-substitution-reduction/dominant-ninth-perfect-fifth-up-rule"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "linear-expressions",
      "references": [
        {
